    "pru"
  ],
  "notes": [
    "AIN0 to AIN6 are 1.8 V maximum, not 3.3 V. Putting 3.3 V on an analog pin destroys the ADC, and it is the single most common way to kill a BeagleBone. Divide every input down.",
    "VADC (pin 32) is the 1.8 V reference and AGND (pin 34) is the analog return. Use those, not a digital GND.",
    "VDD_5V (pins 5 and 6) is only live when the barrel jack is connected. SYS_5V (pins 7 and 8) is the regulated rail and works on USB power too, so capes should take 5 V from there.",
    "I2C2 on pins 19 and 20 is the cape EEPROM bus and already has pull-ups. You can hang other devices on it, but address 0x54 to 0x57 are reserved for cape EEPROMs.",
    "Pins 41 and 42 are each wired to two processor balls. Whichever one you are not using must be set to input, or the two fight each other.",
    "Signal names here are the power-on defaults. Almost every pin can be muxed to something else through the device tree, which is both the strength and the learning curve of this board.",
    "GPIO are 3.3 V and not 5 V tolerant. Source or sink 4 to 6 mA per pin, far less than a Pi.",
    "The other side of this board is the P8 header, documented separately: gwark.dev/openlabbench/pinouts/beaglebone-black-p8"
  ],
